 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "What it does",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part watches its own supply pin. While VDD is below the trip voltage the reset output is asserted, so a microcontroller on the same rail is held in reset during power-up and is put back into reset when the supply sags (brown-out). Inside, a comparator checks VDD against a bandgap reference and feeds a delay circuit and then the output driver. MCP100 asserts by pulling RST low; MCP101 asserts by driving RST high. Both outputs are push-pull."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Why a supervisor",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Two supply faults threaten a microcontroller: a brief drop of the rail below its operating level (brown-out), and a slow decay that leaves it executing code with too little voltage to keep its SRAM valid, so its behaviour becomes unpredictable."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up and reset timing",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "When VDD rises past VTRIP the output stays asserted for tRPU (150 ms min, 350 ms typ, 700 ms max) and then releases. When VDD falls below VTRIP the output asserts again after tRPD, typically 10 µs. The threshold has about 50 mV of hysteresis. The output holds its asserted level with VDD as low as 1.0 V (VDDMIN)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timeout and temperature",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "tRPU comes from an internal RC timer and moves with both supply voltage and temperature: it is longer when cold and at higher VDD. Typical values at 25 °C run from about 275 ms at 3 V to about 380 ms at 6 V (Figure 2-3)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply dips",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "A short negative dip does not reset the part unless it lasts long enough; the deeper the dip below the trip point, the shorter the dip that is enough. At 25 °C a dip 1 V below VTRIP needs about 2.3 µs and a dip 4 V below needs about 0.6 µs (Figure 2-2). A 0.1 µF bypass capacitor placed as close as possible to the VDD pin adds immunity to such dips."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Accuracy",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Over temperature the trip point is held to ±125 mV on the 5 V options and ±75 mV on the 3 V options. Normalised to 25 °C, VTRIP moves by less than 0.6 % between -40 °C and +85 °C (Figure 2-5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Trip voltage options",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Seven trip voltages are offered, coded by the three digits after the device name: 270 (2.55 to 2.70 V), 300 (2.85 to 3.00 V), 315 (3.00 to 3.15 V) for 3 V and 3.3 V systems; 450 (4.25 to 4.50 V), 460 (4.35 to 4.60 V), 475 (4.50 to 4.75 V) and 485 (4.60 to 4.85 V) for 5 V systems."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ordering code",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The code reads device, optional T for tape and reel, trip voltage, bondout (D or H, TO-92 only), temperature range (I, -40 °C to +85 °C, the only range offered) and package (/TO for TO-92 in bags, /TT for SOT-23 on tape and reel only). MCP101-475DI/TO is the active-high part with a 4.50 to 4.75 V trip point, D bondout, in TO-92."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "TO-92 parts carry the part number over up to three lines followed by a year, week and traceability code (YWWNNN); when the part number overflows a line it continues on the next, leaving less room for customer text. SOT-23 parts carry a four-character code XXNN: XX names the part (QJ to QP for MCP100T, RJ to RP for MCP101T, in trip-voltage order) and NN is traceability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Typical curves",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Curve values are typical, from a limited number of samples; they are not tested or guaranteed, and some lie outside the specified operating range, such as VDD = 6 V in Figure 2-3."
  }
 ],
